Recursion is when a function calls itself to solve smaller instances of the same problem.

def factorial(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n - 1)

The base case (n <= 1) stops the recursion; each call reduces n, guaranteeing termination.

A recursive function solves a problem by calling itself with smaller input.

def fib(n):
    if n < 2:
        return n
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)

Note: naive fib is exponential; memoize for real use.

Recursion means a function invokes itself.

def countdown(n):
    if n == 0:
        return
    print(n)
    countdown(n - 1)
